item.text:
Exposure assessment: this is a `limited_exposure_with_choices` case, not a nuclear-arsenal command case. Egypt had civilian nuclear authority, treaty diplomacy, and a live reactor project in 2022, but no public evidence of warhead command, brinkmanship, or operational nuclear use. I am not scoring.

**Reused evidence**
None. There was no accepted web evidence to reuse.

**New evidence**

- [President of the Republic](https://sis.gov.eg/en/egypt/system-of-government/executive-authority/president-of-the-republic/) establishes the office Sisi held in 2022 as head of state and head of executive power. This is basic authority context, not proof of any nuclear decision. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President of the Republic","publisher":"State Information Service","publication_date":"2025-09-24","url":"https://sis.gov.eg/en/egypt/system-of-government/executive-authority/president-of-the-republic/","claim":"The Egyptian president is the head of state and head of executive power under article 139 of the constitution.","locator":"article 139 excerpt","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official constitutional summary from the Egyptian state information service; the office definition is stable across 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Stable constitutional authority in force during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Defines Abdel Fattah el-Sisi’s office, but not any specific 2022 action.","contrary_evidence":["Constitutional authority alone does not show direct control over every security or nuclear decision."],"lenses":["1B.7","1B.8","1B.10"]}

- [IAEA Country List: Egypt](https://ola.iaea.org/Applications/FactSheets/Country/Detail?code=EG) gives the treaty baseline for Egypt’s nuclear governance: NPT safeguards in force, but no CPPNM accession listed on this page. That supports a cautious institutional reading, with both positive safeguards and some gaps. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Country List: Egypt","publisher":"IAEA Office of Legal Affairs","publication_date":"2026-02-24","url":"https://ola.iaea.org/Applications/FactSheets/Country/Detail?code=EG","claim":"Egypt has an NPT safeguards agreement in force with the IAEA, while the page lists several nuclear-security-related conventions as non-party for Egypt.","locator":"Safeguards Agreements table, lines 112-119; Agreements table, lines 34-42","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct IAEA legal-status listing with treaty registration data; good for formal status context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Treaty posture is stable context for 2022, though the page is current rather than a 2022 snapshot.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level treaty posture under Sisi’s government, not a ruler-personal act.","contrary_evidence":["This is a current legal-status page, not a 2022 contemporaneous record.","Non-party status on some conventions is a gap in security architecture, not proof of negligent conduct."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.4","1B.7","1B.10"]}

- [Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons](https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showActionDetails.aspx?objid=080000028012079e) confirms Egypt ratified the NPT in 1981. That is important baseline context for 2022 disarmament diplomacy and safeguards. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons","publisher":"United Nations Treaty Collection","publication_date":"1981-10-30","url":"https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showActionDetails.aspx?objid=080000028012079e","claim":"Egypt ratified the NPT in 1981.","locator":"action details lines 14-23","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN treaty depositary record with direct ratification entry for Egypt.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Stable treaty status relevant to 2022.","ruler_attribution":"A state commitment inherited by and continuing under Sisi’s presidency.","contrary_evidence":["Ratification does not itself prove good-faith implementation in 2022."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}

- [CTBT status](https://treaties.un.org/Pages/ViewDetails.aspx?chapter=26&clang=_en&mtdsg_no=XXVI-4&src=TREATY) shows Egypt was still only a signatory in 2022, not a ratifier. That is the cleanest formal negative on chapter 1B, though Egypt tied its position to broader regional imbalance. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty status page","publisher":"United Nations Treaty Collection","publication_date":"2026-07-22","url":"https://treaties.un.org/Pages/ViewDetails.aspx?chapter=26&clang=_en&mtdsg_no=XXVI-4&src=TREATY","claim":"Egypt is listed among Annex 2 states for the CTBT with a signature entry but no ratification entry on the status table.","locator":"status lines 87-105 and Egypt row in participant table","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN depositary status table is authoritative for treaty signature and ratification status.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Current status reflects the fact that Egypt had not ratified the CTBT in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"State-level posture during Sisi’s presidency; not evidence of a personal operational nuclear choice.","contrary_evidence":["Egypt publicly framed the non-ratification as linked to regional nonproliferation imbalance and universalization problems, not a desire to test nuclear weapons."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}

- [Sisi hails Dabaa nuclear power plant as key cooperation project with Russia](https://sis.gov.eg/en/media-center/news/sisi-hails-dabaa-nuclear-power-plant-as-key-cooperation-project-with-russia/) is the strongest ruler-attributed 2022 source for his civilian nuclear posture. It shows him publicly sponsoring a peaceful nuclear-energy project, not a weapons program. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Sisi hails Dabaa nuclear power plant as key cooperation project with Russia","publisher":"State Information Service","publication_date":"2022-06-17","url":"https://sis.gov.eg/en/media-center/news/sisi-hails-dabaa-nuclear-power-plant-as-key-cooperation-project-with-russia/","claim":"Sisi publicly described the Dabaa nuclear power plant as an important cooperation project with Russia and part of Egypt's strategy for expansion into new and renewable energy projects.","locator":"article body on the president's virtual speech at SPIEF","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct Egyptian presidential messaging from 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler speech and sponsorship of the project.","contrary_evidence":["This is a development and energy frame, not evidence of nuclear-risk restraint.","It also deepens dependence on Russian cooperation."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.7","1B.10"]}

- [Construction permit issued for first Egyptian unit](https://nppa.gov.eg/News/Details?id=572) documents the regulatory approval before construction, including the safety prerequisite language and Rosatom training/fuel responsibilities. This matters for command-and-control and institutional risk management, but it remains a civilian infrastructure step. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Construction permit issued for first Egyptian unit","publisher":"Nuclear Power Plants Authority","publication_date":"2022-07-01","url":"https://nppa.gov.eg/News/Details?id=572","claim":"ENRRA issued a construction permit for El-Dabaa Unit 1 and the notice says the permit and excavation works were prerequisites for the start of the main stage of construction.","locator":"2022/07/01 notice body","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary regulator/owner notice with dated licensing details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Shows the state apparatus under Sisi advancing a civilian nuclear project through licensing, not personal command over weapons.","contrary_evidence":["The notice is self-promotional and does not independently verify long-term safety performance.","It evidences construction approval, not whether internal oversight was optimal."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.10"]}

- [Rosatom gets green light from Egypt to build Dabaa nuclear plant](https://www.marketscreener.com/news/latest/Rosatom-gets-green-light-from-Egypt-to-build-Dabaa-nuclear-plant-40858513/) adds an independent Reuters line: the Egyptian regulator granted permission for Rosatom to start building the country’s first nuclear plant. This confirms the official permit story from outside the Egyptian government. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rosatom gets green light from Egypt to build Dabaa nuclear plant","publisher":"Reuters via MarketScreener","publication_date":"2022-06-29","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/news/latest/Rosatom-gets-green-light-from-Egypt-to-build-Dabaa-nuclear-plant-40858513/","claim":"Rosatom said it received permission from an Egyptian regulator to start building Egypt's first nuclear plant at Dabaa.","locator":"Reuters lede in the syndicated story","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting is strong independent confirmation, though this is a syndication page rather than Reuters-hosted original.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"State-level regulatory action under Sisi's government.","contrary_evidence":["This confirms permission to build, not safe operation or ruler-level prudence by itself."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.10"]}

- [Construction of Egypt's first nuclear power plant under way](https://www.world-nuclear-news.org/Articles/Construction-of-Egypts-first-nuclear-power-plant-u) gives the first-concrete milestone in July 2022 and shows the plant under ENRRA licensing. That is strong civilian nuclear-program evidence, not weapons evidence. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Construction of Egypt's first nuclear power plant under way","publisher":"World Nuclear News","publication_date":"2022-07-20","url":"https://www.world-nuclear-news.org/Articles/Construction-of-Egypts-first-nuclear-power-plant-u","claim":"The first safety-related concrete was poured for El Dabaa Unit 1, marking the official start of construction after ENRRA issued the unit 1 construction licence.","locator":"article lede and middle paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Industry publication with dated construction and licensing details; strong for project chronology, weaker for political attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Confirms a civilian nuclear project advanced during Sisi's presidency, not a weapons decision.","contrary_evidence":["The source is industry-focused and may emphasize project success.","It does not independently evaluate security governance or accident risk."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.10"]}

- [Egypt begins construction of first reactor at El-Dabaa nuclear plant](https://english.ahram.org.eg/NewsContent/3/16/471838/Business/Energy/Egypt-begins-construction-of-first-reactor-at-ElDa.aspx) is useful because it adds Egyptian press confirmation plus the explicit IAEA-safety framing and ENRRA permit mention. It is still a civilian power-program story, but it strengthens the institutional picture. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt begins construction of first reactor at El-Dabaa nuclear plant","publisher":"Ahram Online","publication_date":"2022-07-20","url":"https://english.ahram.org.eg/NewsContent/3/16/471838/Business/Energy/Egypt-begins-construction-of-first-reactor-at-ElDa.aspx","claim":"Egypt began construction of the first reactor at El-Dabaa, and the article says the reactors comply with IAEA safety measurements and that ENRRA permitted Rosatom to start construction.","locator":"article body paragraphs 55-82","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reputable Egyptian outlet with concrete project and safety details, but still secondary reporting.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Reflects the state project under Sisi, but not a personal nuclear-safety decision by him.","contrary_evidence":["The article repeats official assurances on safety rather than independently auditing them.","It reports project launch, not operational safety outcomes."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.10"]}

- [Egypt's delegate in New York stresses importance of fulfilling obligations on nuclear disarmament](https://sis.gov.eg/en/media-center/news/egypts-delegate-in-new-york-stresses-importance-of-fulfilling-obligations-on-nuclear-disarmament/) shows Egypt’s 2022 diplomatic line: nuclear-weapon states should fulfill disarmament obligations, and the Middle East should become a nuclear-weapon-free zone. This is favorable rhetoric and diplomacy, but it is not operational restraint. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt's delegate in New York stresses importance of fulfilling obligations on nuclear disarmament","publisher":"State Information Service","publication_date":"2022-08-05","url":"https://sis.gov.eg/en/media-center/news/egypts-delegate-in-new-york-stresses-importance-of-fulfilling-obligations-on-nuclear-disarmament/","claim":"Egypt said nuclear-weapon states should fulfill their disarmament obligations and that measures to reduce nuclear risk should not substitute for nuclear disarmament, while reaffirming support for a Middle East nuclear-weapon-free zone.","locator":"article body and quoted mission statement","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct 2022 Egyptian diplomatic statement published by the state information service.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"State position during Sisi's presidency; not a personal operational control decision.","contrary_evidence":["This is diplomatic advocacy, not evidence that Sisi changed Egypt's own risk posture in a measurable way."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.4","1B.9"]}

- [False logic of deterrence elevates role of nuclear weapons in security doctrines](https://press.un.org/en/2022/gadis3684.doc.htm) captures Egypt’s official 2022 line at the UN: it condemned the increased salience of nuclear weapons, called disarmament a legal obligation, and rejected normalization of nuclear threats. This is one of the clearest favorable sources for 1B.2 and 1B.4. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"False logic of deterrence elevates role of nuclear weapons in security doctrines, First Committee hears as general debate continues","publisher":"UN Press","publication_date":"2022-10-06","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/gadis3684.doc.htm","claim":"Egypt said nuclear disarmament is a legal obligation and condemned all nuclear threats, explicit or implicit.","locator":"First Committee meeting record, Egypt intervention","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN meeting coverage gives a contemporaneous record of Egypt's official statement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"State diplomacy under Sisi, not a private or military command action.","contrary_evidence":["Rhetorical opposition to threats does not prove equally strong action on safeguards or treaty ratification."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.4","1B.8"]}

- [Non-Proliferation Treaty Review Conference Ends without Adopting Substantive Outcome Document](https://press.un.org/en/2022/dc3850.doc.htm) gives a useful contrary-and-favorable mix: Egypt, speaking for the New Agenda Coalition, said the draft did not restore the balance between disarmament and nonproliferation and reiterated article VI obligations. This shows a consistent legalist posture, but also a continued insistence on the regional imbalance rationale. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Non-Proliferation Treaty Review Conference Ends without Adopting Substantive Outcome Document Due to Opposition by One Member State","publisher":"UN Press","publication_date":"2022-08-26","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/dc3850.doc.htm","claim":"Egypt, speaking for the New Agenda Coalition, said the draft outcome document failed to restore the balance of article VI obligations and reiterated the legal duty to pursue nuclear disarmament.","locator":"meeting summary paragraph on Egypt/New Agenda Coalition intervention","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN meeting coverage of Egypt's statement during the 2022 NPT review conference.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"State line under Sisi, not a direct presidential operational order.","contrary_evidence":["The statement is about global treaty politics, not concrete Egyptian implementation.","It does not address whether Egypt improved its own safeguards posture in 2022."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}

- [Non-nuclear-armed states get nothing in return for fulfilling commitments except threat of potential annihilation](https://press.un.org/en/2022/gadis3690.doc.htm) is the best source for Egypt’s explicit CTBT rationale in 2022. Egypt said it had signed the treaty but had not continued because of imbalance in the regime and because some regional nuclear facilities remained outside comprehensive IAEA safeguards. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Non-Nuclear-Armed States Get Nothing in Return for Fulfilling Commitments Except Threat of Potential Annihilation, First Committee Told","publisher":"UN Press","publication_date":"2022-10-06","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/gadis3690.doc.htm","claim":"Egypt said it signed the CTBT but had not continued toward ratification because of ongoing imbalance in the nuclear regime, especially in the Middle East, and because some facilities remained outside comprehensive IAEA safeguards.","locator":"Egypt right-of-reply section in the meeting record","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN record of Egypt's own explanation, contemporaneous with 2022 treaty debate.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"State explanation under Sisi's presidency; useful for attribution but still a foreign-policy stance rather than a direct nuclear-risk act.","contrary_evidence":["This is self-justifying state rhetoric and may overstate the imbalance rationale.","It confirms non-ratification in 2022 rather than active disarmament implementation."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.6","1B.10"]}

- [Conference on the Establishment of a Middle East Zone Free of Nuclear Weapons and Other Weapons of Mass Destruction](https://www.un.org/en/unpdf/conference-on-establishment-of-middle-east-zone-free-of-nuclear-weapons-and-other-wmd) anchors the inherited regional baseline: Egypt was part of the long-running process and the 2022 third session was an active diplomatic track. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Conference on the Establishment of a Middle East Zone Free of Nuclear Weapons and Other Weapons of Mass Destruction","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2022-11-14","url":"https://www.un.org/en/unpdf/conference-on-establishment-of-middle-east-zone-free-of-nuclear-weapons-and-other-wmd","claim":"The UN lists the third session of the Middle East zone conference as scheduled for 14 to 18 November 2022, reflecting the ongoing regional process Egypt supported.","locator":"conference background and session list","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN background page for the conference process and its 2022 session schedule.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit for the diplomatic process.","ruler_attribution":"Regional posture under Sisi’s government, not an individual weapon-safety decision.","contrary_evidence":["A diplomatic process is not the same as successful implementation.","The page does not prove Egypt moved from advocacy to substantive regional breakthrough in 2022."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.8","1B.10"]}

- [Letter dated 12 April 1996 from the Permanent Representative of Egypt](https://www.un.org/unispal/document/auto-insert-186195/) is older, but it is the cleanest inherited-baseline source for Egypt’s long-running nuclear-free-zone posture. It matters because 2022 conduct is partly constrained by this longstanding diplomatic line. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Letter dated 12 April 1996 from the Permanent Representative of Egypt","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"1996-04-12","url":"https://www.un.org/unispal/document/auto-insert-186195/","claim":"Egypt transmitted the Cairo Declaration adopted on the occasion of the signature of the African Nuclear-Weapon-Free Zone Treaty and reiterated support for nuclear-weapon-free arrangements.","locator":"Cairo Declaration annex and transmittal letter","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN record of Egypt's own 1996 diplomatic position.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Inherited baseline rather than 2022 direct conduct, but relevant to attribution.","ruler_attribution":"Historical state posture inherited by later Egyptian governments, including Sisi's.","contrary_evidence":["This is historical context, not 2022 evidence of Sisi's own conduct.","A longstanding norm does not itself prove present-day restraint."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**

I reviewed roughly 30 documents across Egyptian official pages, IAEA/UN treaty records, UN press coverage, Reuters syndication, World Nuclear News, Ahram Online, Egypt Today, Xinhua, CTBTO explainers, and regional disarmament pages.

I rejected or de-emphasized:
- Duplicate Dabaa coverage that repeated the same permit or first-concrete event without adding a materially distinct claim.
- Generic CTBTO FAQ/explainer pages when the UN treaty database already gave the cleaner treaty-status record.
- Broad 2023-2026 retrospectives that were useful for context but not necessary for a 2022 chapter dossier.
- Sources that were too generic, too off-topic, or not Egypt-specific enough to justify inclusion.
- One or two official Egyptian pages that were useful only as snippets because the page fetched with a 502 on open; I relied on the snippet only when it added a unique factual point.
- Old or historical disarmament records when a more direct 2022 source existed, except for the inherited-baseline Cairo Declaration, which is genuinely useful for attribution.

**Remaining gaps**

- Direct evidence of Sisi personally making any nuclear-safety, command-and-control, or crisis-escalation choice in 2022 is thin. Egypt had civilian nuclear governance, not a nuclear arsenal.
- I did not find strong 2022 evidence on whether Egypt had an Additional Protocol in force or on any strengthened safeguards beyond the basic NPT agreement.
- The public record is sparse on internal nuclear-security staffing, emergency planning, or cyber/physical protection decisions, so 1B.3 and 1B.7 remain only moderately evidenced.
- 1B.9 remains weak: I found general Egyptian references to cyber and outer-space risk in UN rhetoric, but not a ruler-specific, programmatic 2022 policy on dual-use catastrophic-risk governance.
- More search could add nuance on treaty diplomacy, but it is unlikely to change the core exposure classification: Egypt in 2022 was a limited-exposure civilian nuclear and nonproliferation case, not a weapon-command or near-use case.
